Original commit body from 701bb2172b7ae7fec3a26f5df1c013e929f3b2b2:

Both shipped in March with tests and 100% coverage and have produced ZERO
DDG edges ever since. Four independent things must all hold before an
extractor has any effect, and each alone is enough to make it silently
inert: the CFG mapping must declare atomic_statement; a LanguageDdgSpec
must be registered; the registering module must be imported on the
production path, since registration is an import side effect; and the
chain must actually yield an edge. Rust and TypeScript failed the first
three simultaneously.

Fixing three sites by hand would leave the next language to rediscover the
same trap, so test_ddg_language_wiring.py asserts all of it for EVERY
language with a registered extractor. Red-first it failed 7 of 14. The
production import list stays explicit -- greppable, import cost visible --
but it is a second home for a fact the filesystem already holds, so an
exhaustiveness test fails when the two disagree.

Measured: rust and typescript 0 -> 2 DDG edges on the identical fixture,
and stripping atomic_statement returns them to 0. Real repos: zoxide
273 edges / 72 symbols, apollo-server 1176 / 91.

ATOMIC_STATEMENT SWALLOWED RUST'S CONTROL FLOW, and the fix is in the
builder rather than the mapping. Declaring expression_statement atomic --
which Rust must, to get def/use on bare calls -- broke three CFG tests,
because tree-sitter parses `if c {} else {}` as expression_statement >
if_expression and `let x = f()?;` as let_declaration > try_expression. The
declaration stopped the descent before the branch was classified, so the
CFG lost its true/false edges while every def/use measurement kept working
-- the wrapper had been declared FOR def/use. Statement-oriented grammars
cannot hit this; expression-oriented ones hit it routinely. CfgBuilder now
honours atomic_statement only when the subtree holds no control-flow
descendant, deciding per occurrence rather than per node type, which is all
a YAML mapping can express.

Impact measured, not assumed: Python identical (61,113 edges / 6,946
symbols on pretix), Go caddy 28,716/1,635 -> 28,650/1,678, Rust zoxide
297/86 -> 273/72. Only Python feeds taint, so no verdict can move.

The new gate did NOT catch that regression -- edge counts cannot see a
swallowed branch -- so it gained a branch-preservation check, mutation
proved to redden Rust alone. The containment check recurses, and a
one-level fixture leaves that unexercised, so test_cfg gains a nested case
(let_declaration > call_expression > arguments > if_expression).

COHORT A/B, 9 repos, re-run after the builder guard: 54 verdict rows -> 54,
270 displayed evidence rows -> 270, 311 total flows -> 311, 38 confirmed /
16 violated unchanged, 0 cells differ across full evidence tuples. Nothing
reads Rust or TypeScript DDG edges yet, so movement would have meant a leak
rather than an improvement -- Python's propagator receives the whole-repo
edge list, not a language-filtered one. The arm's instrument asserts both
directions before measuring: that the wiring is live, and that foreign
symbol ids stay disjoint from Python seed ids via the language prefix,
which is WHY the null is expected by construction.

DISCLOSED, because the catalog points the other way. The glob is *.ts, so
JavaScript gets nothing (express: 0 edges) -- and JavaScript carries 50
sources and 83 SINKS where TypeScript carries 6 and none. typescript.yaml
claims in its own header to also cover JavaScript, but it is keyed to
`typescript` and the extractor registers only for `typescript`, so no .js
file reaches any of it. This registration is therefore NOT a taint
improvement and is not presented as one. Class methods are excluded too,
because the analyzer decides their kind slot inline and re-deriving it here
would put a second copy of a production classification in a consumer.

select_ddg_targets' docstring claimed "only 2-10% of functions are
analyzed" while the function has zero production callers and build_repo_ddg
walks every function with no sampling, budget or cache -- corrected, and
its wiring deliberately sequenced after 3a, since it reduces which
functions carry DDG data and would make 3a's effect unreadable.

Scope stated as data: the gate is blind by construction to a language with
no extractor at all, so it is a regression guard, not a coverage claim.
javascript (83 sinks, nothing wired) and java (69 sinks, a mapping but no
extractor) are outside its population and named in place.

